  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nThrough Tuesday: Through Monday: Storms have once again developed\nwith strong daytime heating and are mainly focused over the \nwestern half of the area. A favorable environment for microbursts\nwith vertical totals near 26 deg C and DCAPE near 1500 j/kg is \nsupporting a few intense storms with strong wind gusts, and\nwouldn't rule out a marginally severe microburst or two. There \nare currently numerous outflow boundaries supporting greater storm\ncoverage as well, and this storm threat should continue to the \nearly evening hours. The storm threat for Tuesday should less\nquite a bit and be confined to far southern portions of the area.\nOtherwise, dangerous heat continues to be the primary concern, \nand the heat advisory was extended to Tuesday, although it was\ntrimmed a bit from today's more expansive area. /EC/\n\nWednesday through next Monday: Continuing easterly flow will \nusher in a relatively drier airmass through the end of the work \nweek. This airmass will still support hot days, with temps still \napproaching the triple digits in the ArkLaMiss Delta. But with \ndewpoints dropping into 60s and perhaps the 50s, heat stress will \nbecome less of a concern through the end of the week. It is still \npossible a heat advisory may be needed for some areas mainly west \nof I-55 on Wednesday, but heat related alerts are unlikely for the\nremainder of the week. While days will still be quite warm with \nabove normal temps, mornings will see temps dipping into the 60s \nin many areas. Deep layer moisture will begin to increase again \nthis weekend, with returning potential for diurnal convection each\nday. Though it will still be warm, high temps are expected to \nregress closer to seasonal normals around the beginning of next \nweek. /DL/EC/",
